# FindMyPast targets — everyone this archive cannot account for.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The trial of 13-14 September 2026 ended with a list of
# surnames it would not touch: «Perković (2,036), Borić (2,007), Papić (1,042),
# Prpić (943), Boras (3,445) are too common to harvest in bulk. THEY NEED A
# FIRST NAME AND A YEAR.» This is the first name and the year. It is generated
# from the archive rather than typed, so it cannot drift from the tree.
#
# WHO IS IN IT. Every published person born 1840-1910 with NO DEATH RECORDED —
# the cohort old enough to sail and young enough to be on a post-1906 manifest,
# whose story this archive loses. 386 people. It is not a list of emigrants; it
# is a list of people the archive stops following, which is the same list until
# somebody looks.
#
# HOW TO WORK IT. Surname first, not person first. One surname search read end
# to end beats forty name searches, and the trial proved why: ZUBRINICH returns
# 191 records and gives AUSTRALIA, ZUBRINIC returns a DIFFERENT 119 and gives
# LIKA. The «spellings» column carries every variant this archive has met.
#
# TWO THINGS THE TRIAL LEARNED THAT SAVE A DAY.
#   · Only the pre-1925 Ellis manifests (ELLIS2 transcripts) carry a LAST
#     RESIDENCE and the relative left behind. That residence is the whole value:
#     it is what turned the Kalanji from a Klenovica family into Brinje people.
#   · Ids ending «…/2» and «…/3» are RELATIONS NAMED ON a record, not passengers.
#
# AND ONE NEGATIVE, SO IT IS NOT RE-TESTED. FindMyPast holds NO Croatian parish
# registers, and nothing on Hedviga's 1948 emigration: no Hedviga Blazevic
# anywhere, no Defranceski in Passenger Lists Leaving UK 1890-1960. They went to
# South Africa and this service has neither end of the voyage.
#
# surname|given|born|birthplace|slug|ahn|spellings to try
